1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,800
This is ChangeLock — a verification checkpoint
for vendor bank-detail changes.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,600
Everything you are about to see
is deterministic replay.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:12,350
No live calls are placed,
and none are claimed.

4
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:17,500
Four vendor payment-change requests
sit in the inbox.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,000
Every one of them is held —

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,120
ChangeLock can never approve a payment change,
only verify the story behind it.

7
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:29,500
Open the Northstar case,
and the trust rail tells the whole story.

8
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:34,500
On the left, the untrusted request —
the name and number inside the request itself,

9
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,000
treated as attacker-controlled,
masked, and never dialed.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
In the middle, the independent callback —
the trusted contact already in the vendor master.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,500
That is the only number
ChangeLock will ever call.

12
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:51,630
On the right, evidence only —
whatever the call learns, the payment stays held.

13
00:00:52,630 --> 00:00:55,500
The call itself is a bounded contract.

14
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:00,000
An automation disclosure up front.
One question: did you request this change.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,500
No bank details. No credentials.
No one-time passcodes.

16
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:06,800
It stops on refusal.

17
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:09,500
And it closes with the line that matters:

18
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:12,650
this call does not approve anything.

19
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:16,500
Before anything runs,
a human authorizes it.

20
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:24,060
The operator picks the scenario, types the case
verification phrase, and attests the contact consented.

21
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:30,000
Here the replay comes back denied —
the vendor says they never asked for this change.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,500
The denial is recorded as evidence,
bound to this request.

23
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,200
And the payment change stays held.

24
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:39,330
An analyst now has proof, not a hunch.

25
00:01:40,330 --> 00:01:48,500
Every result must pass binding gates: same call, same
intent, same request hash, same trusted destination.

26
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:54,690
If any gate fails, the result is rejected
and the case fails closed.

27
00:01:55,690 --> 00:01:59,000
On a second case, the replay
comes back confirmed.

28
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Green evidence — the vendor did request it.

29
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,500
And notice: the payment change is still held.

30
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:10,580
Confirmed is evidence, not approval.
A human decides what happens next.

31
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:17,500
And when verification cannot complete — unreachable,
malformed, ambiguous — ChangeLock fails closed.

32
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:22,500
In our own local live test, one real authorized call
was placed to a consented test contact.

33
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:24,700
It reached no answer.

34
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:29,840
No answer is not proof either way.
The request stayed held.

35
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:38,980
Behind it all, a hash-linked audit timeline records
every action, every gate, every masked number.

36
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:46,500
One bounded question, to the number
you already trusted —

37
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:49,500
producing evidence a human can act on.

38
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:54,040
The call auditors keep telling companies to make —
finally enforced by the system itself.
